00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:47.440
Dan.

00:19:47.440 --> 00:19:51.080
Hey, hi everybody.

00:19:51.080 --> 00:19:52.970
First of all, it's
so amazing to see

00:19:52.970 --> 00:19:56.160
all the work the dance teams are
doing that you all are making

00:19:56.160 --> 00:19:57.200
and the videos.

00:19:57.200 --> 00:20:03.170
It's super inspiring, and
I feel like hard to follow,

00:20:03.170 --> 00:20:07.520
so I will do my best, but
we'll see what happens.

00:20:07.520 --> 00:20:11.390
I've been-- I'm a lecturer in
movement and dance and theater

00:20:11.390 --> 00:20:17.990
arts, and I have been focused
mostly on what I make going,

00:20:17.990 --> 00:20:19.790
how do you turn--

00:20:19.790 --> 00:20:22.700
I like this idea how do you
turn the scar into a star,

00:20:22.700 --> 00:20:26.180
so how do we take the problem
and turn it into an asset.

00:20:26.180 --> 00:20:27.830
And looking at how
we can make dance

00:20:27.830 --> 00:20:34.640
that is served by this bizarre
video teleconference situation

00:20:34.640 --> 00:20:38.720
that we're in, and how we can
make things that are not trying

00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:41.210
to approximate what
we would have done

00:20:41.210 --> 00:20:47.680
live, but make things that
can only be done with video.

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:50.120
And I've been involved
in a number of projects--

00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:50.810
oddly since--

00:20:50.810 --> 00:20:57.200
when COVID hit, I was just about
to start rehearsing a show.

00:20:57.200 --> 00:21:00.110
I was choreographing a
production at Bard College,

00:21:00.110 --> 00:21:01.910
and they immediately
just switched it

00:21:01.910 --> 00:21:03.370
to an online production.

00:21:03.370 --> 00:21:05.100
So I had one
rehearsal in person,

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:08.900
then everything after
that was virtual.

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:11.180
So I've been getting
a lot of experience

00:21:11.180 --> 00:21:16.040
making stuff via Zoom and
how to work asynchronously

00:21:16.040 --> 00:21:17.720
and how to make
stuff that will be

00:21:17.720 --> 00:21:19.730
effective for a video format.

00:21:19.730 --> 00:21:23.480
And then I was really lucky to
work with this choreographer

00:21:23.480 --> 00:21:27.020
and filmmaker named Janessa
Clark, who's a New York based

00:21:27.020 --> 00:21:31.603
artist, who has this project
called Communion, where she's

00:21:31.603 --> 00:21:33.770
getting different people
to film themselves dancing,

00:21:33.770 --> 00:21:37.100
and then she's compositing
them into duets.

00:21:37.100 --> 00:21:39.830
And there's an artist
named Benoit Lachambre

00:21:39.830 --> 00:21:43.040
who's a Montreal based
choreographer and dancer who--

00:21:43.040 --> 00:21:46.400
he and I've been like sort
of on each other's radar

00:21:46.400 --> 00:21:48.710
for about 15 years, and I've
wanted to work with him.

00:21:48.710 --> 00:21:50.700
He's one of my favorite artists.

00:21:50.700 --> 00:21:52.490
I've wanted to work
with him forever,

00:21:52.490 --> 00:21:54.560
and suddenly with
this situation,

00:21:54.560 --> 00:21:57.680
it was an opportunity for
us to make a film together.

00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:01.230
So Miguel, if you want
to roll that video

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:03.130
of the duet of
Benoit and I. This

00:22:03.130 --> 00:22:05.370
is just a little bit
of a longer piece.

00:22:09.156 --> 00:23:01.040
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:23:01.040 --> 00:23:02.260
So yeah, you get the idea.

00:23:02.260 --> 00:23:08.880
So yeah, with that
project, what we did was we

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:09.970
both worked individually.

00:23:09.970 --> 00:23:12.690
I went first filming,
and Janessa set us up

00:23:12.690 --> 00:23:15.510
in very specific ways, like we
made parameters that worked.

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:18.090
The camera has a certain
height off the floor

00:23:18.090 --> 00:23:19.800
and a certain height
from the back wall,

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:24.120
the back wall had to be white,
so we could match them up.

00:23:24.120 --> 00:23:28.740
We both improvised too, either--

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:29.370
we did a bunch.

00:23:29.370 --> 00:23:32.250
We both improvised to [? a ?]
[? Bowie ?] [? song or ?]

00:23:32.250 --> 00:23:36.090
to Erik Satie, but
then cut the sound out.

00:23:36.090 --> 00:23:40.170
And then I went first, then
and while I watched mine,

00:23:40.170 --> 00:23:42.540
he kind of made his
a response to mine.

00:23:42.540 --> 00:23:44.700
And then actually my
video got messed up,

00:23:44.700 --> 00:23:46.530
so I had to make a
response to Benoit.

00:23:46.530 --> 00:23:49.080
So we kind of did
like a back and forth

00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:52.350
to influence each
other, and then

00:23:52.350 --> 00:23:55.350
put all that together and
then sent that-- and then

00:23:55.350 --> 00:23:57.660
Janessa sent that off
to a composer, who

00:23:57.660 --> 00:23:59.460
wrote brand new music for it.

00:23:59.460 --> 00:24:02.160
That was really a process like
layer upon layer upon layer

00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:03.272
upon layer upon layer.

00:24:03.272 --> 00:24:04.980
And we found that that
was actually a way

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:08.030
that we could work
that would like

00:24:08.030 --> 00:24:11.930
was only possible via the
distance and the video

00:24:11.930 --> 00:24:14.028
and stuff.

00:24:14.028 --> 00:24:16.070
And so I'm looking, I'm
really excited about ways

00:24:16.070 --> 00:24:19.670
that we can keep exploring that
rely on the video as opposed

00:24:19.670 --> 00:24:21.380
to use it as a hindrance.

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:24.500
Awesome.

00:24:24.500 --> 00:24:26.020
Thank you, Dan.

00:24:26.020 --> 00:24:27.950
Yes, thank you.

