00:25:27.120 --> 00:25:29.230
and they're all
like really cool.

00:25:29.230 --> 00:25:30.870
The one you saw that
was on the Charles

00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:32.070
River, that was on the dock.

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:35.520
Awesome.

00:25:35.520 --> 00:25:38.355
And so also, who does the
styling for your videos?

00:25:40.960 --> 00:25:42.100
Who does the styling?

00:25:42.100 --> 00:25:44.770
Normally, in the
past we've actually

00:25:44.770 --> 00:25:47.830
hired professional video editors
for some of our like bigger

00:25:47.830 --> 00:25:50.290
and more time intensive covers.

00:25:50.290 --> 00:25:54.250
In the pa-- we've also just had
like specific cover directors

00:25:54.250 --> 00:25:56.920
and choreographers
edit their own videos,

00:25:56.920 --> 00:26:00.820
but they're all filmed and
edited really well, I think.

00:26:00.820 --> 00:26:01.900
Awesome.

00:26:01.900 --> 00:26:05.740
And so a great
question was posed that

00:26:05.740 --> 00:26:08.350
with the time of being
and [? with ?] everybody

00:26:08.350 --> 00:26:12.490
doing on Zoom how do you
manage synchronization issues?

00:26:12.490 --> 00:26:13.840
Open to anybody.

00:26:13.840 --> 00:26:15.340
I mean, I can jump
on that, and then

00:26:15.340 --> 00:26:18.460
I would welcome anybody
to correct me or augment

00:26:18.460 --> 00:26:20.000
what I say.

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:22.990
I found that starting
to work and not

00:26:22.990 --> 00:26:26.605
being so worried about synced
up is a great way to start.

00:26:26.605 --> 00:26:28.480
Just going like, we're
just generating moves,

00:26:28.480 --> 00:26:31.220
we're just making stuff, and
we're allowed to get it wrong.

00:26:31.220 --> 00:26:34.750
And then when you hit a certain
point of having to sync it up,

00:26:34.750 --> 00:26:37.800
at that point I rely
on one person who--

00:26:37.800 --> 00:26:42.190
if I'm choreographing, I kind of
pick whoever is like nailing it

00:26:42.190 --> 00:26:46.180
and go, OK you record
it and make a video.

00:26:46.180 --> 00:26:49.420
And then we're all going
to asynchronously match up

00:26:49.420 --> 00:26:52.910
to your moves on this song
and learn it that way.

00:26:52.910 --> 00:26:54.550
So at least that way you can--

00:26:54.550 --> 00:26:57.310
you're not trying to get
synchronous on a Zoom call,

00:26:57.310 --> 00:26:58.810
you're doing it asynchronously.

00:26:58.810 --> 00:27:01.000
And then, to be quite
honest, you just

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:03.820
have to hope that there's
like not awful latency issues

00:27:03.820 --> 00:27:06.550
if you're performing it live,
or you can do it in the edit

00:27:06.550 --> 00:27:09.200
if you're doing it not live.

00:27:09.200 --> 00:27:11.410
But it's allowing
for the problems

00:27:11.410 --> 00:27:15.610
to be there until you need to
pin it down, has been effective

00:27:15.610 --> 00:27:17.540
for me.

00:27:17.540 --> 00:27:20.425
And I'd love to hear
other solutions.

00:27:20.425 --> 00:27:23.830
Yeah, so I think one thing
I've experienced as well

00:27:23.830 --> 00:27:28.550
is like I guess the asynchronous
problem is a problem.

00:27:28.550 --> 00:27:31.780
But if one person is
also sharing their audio,

00:27:31.780 --> 00:27:35.800
like as if they're playing
the music for everyone else,

00:27:35.800 --> 00:27:38.940
then at least for them the audio
and the movement is synced up.

00:27:38.940 --> 00:27:41.440
So then you're going to have
like one person sort of lead it

00:27:41.440 --> 00:27:46.980
in a way, which allows for
some amount of synchronization.

00:27:46.980 --> 00:27:49.980
Yeah, for us
specifically we split up

00:27:49.980 --> 00:27:52.930
like sections of the song
we want to choreograph,

00:27:52.930 --> 00:27:56.680
and basically like he
took the first 20 seconds,

00:27:56.680 --> 00:27:59.470
and then I took the next
20 seconds, and so forth.

00:27:59.470 --> 00:28:02.260
And in that way, we were all--
we had our goals in terms

00:28:02.260 --> 00:28:04.750
of the section of the
song, and it was easier

00:28:04.750 --> 00:28:06.230
to sync up that way.

00:28:06.230 --> 00:28:08.830
Right, yeah, and when we were
sort of making our video,

00:28:08.830 --> 00:28:12.490
I guess we met up more to
discuss like high level ideas,

00:28:12.490 --> 00:28:15.520
like structural ideas and
then we kind of split off

00:28:15.520 --> 00:28:19.404
to implement like
specific choreo and so--

00:28:19.404 --> 00:28:21.220
Awesome.

00:28:21.220 --> 00:28:21.745
Yeah.

00:28:21.745 --> 00:28:23.620
In terms of the
synchronization, I would just

00:28:23.620 --> 00:28:26.530
add a note like sometimes
if your Wi-Fi is bad,

00:28:26.530 --> 00:28:28.240
and you share your
computer video

00:28:28.240 --> 00:28:30.190
along with the audio,
that actually makes

00:28:30.190 --> 00:28:31.720
the synchronization worse.

00:28:31.720 --> 00:28:34.810
So there's actually an option
to share computer audio only,

00:28:34.810 --> 00:28:36.820
and that will sync
it up with whoever

00:28:36.820 --> 00:28:38.822
is sharing [? this ?] video.

00:28:38.822 --> 00:28:40.030
So that works for Zoom calls.

00:28:40.030 --> 00:28:42.480
And also when we're
video editing,

00:28:42.480 --> 00:28:43.960
and everyone plays
their own music,

00:28:43.960 --> 00:28:45.550
there is an option
in the software

00:28:45.550 --> 00:28:49.270
we use that actually allows
like automatic synchronization

00:28:49.270 --> 00:28:50.818
of the different frames.

00:28:50.818 --> 00:28:52.360
So that's really,
really handy, so we

00:28:52.360 --> 00:28:55.460
don't have to do it by eye.

00:28:55.460 --> 00:28:56.330
Awesome.

00:28:56.330 --> 00:28:57.380
We're going to get
to a specific one,

00:28:57.380 --> 00:28:59.255
and I'm going to expand
it just a little bit.

00:28:59.255 --> 00:29:02.360
So Dan, how was the process
for choreographing the duet

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:04.310
and editing the film like?

00:29:04.310 --> 00:29:06.580
And then I also want to hear
from Joy and from Faraaz

00:29:06.580 --> 00:29:09.190
and Aniket how you guys
dealt with video editing.

00:29:09.190 --> 00:29:11.290
Dan?

00:29:11.290 --> 00:29:14.320
For that project I actually
didn't do the edit.

00:29:14.320 --> 00:29:16.990
Janessa, whose project
it is, did the edit.

00:29:16.990 --> 00:29:19.390
A lot of it was about
scale and make--

00:29:19.390 --> 00:29:21.040
because we had the
bodies separately,

00:29:21.040 --> 00:29:23.950
we had to make sure the
parameters of filming

00:29:23.950 --> 00:29:28.133
were nailed down,
so that like we

00:29:28.133 --> 00:29:29.800
actually measured on
the wall and showed

00:29:29.800 --> 00:29:31.990
where 6 feet was at one point.

00:29:31.990 --> 00:29:34.000
I'm a little taller
than Benoit, so we

00:29:34.000 --> 00:29:36.810
like we wanted to get
our actual heights even,

00:29:36.810 --> 00:29:39.410
and then you can kind of let
the camera roll from there.

00:29:39.410 --> 00:29:41.740
But a lot of it was all
about setup beforehand

00:29:41.740 --> 00:29:47.560
and like allowing for as much
variation in the movement

00:29:47.560 --> 00:29:50.003
as possible, but in
really strict parameters.

00:29:50.003 --> 00:29:52.420
So it's sort of like how do
you troubleshoot it before you

00:29:52.420 --> 00:29:54.408
do it, not after the fact.

00:29:54.408 --> 00:29:56.200
That, I think, is a
really important aspect

00:29:56.200 --> 00:29:59.020
of this kind of making
stuff like this.

00:29:59.020 --> 00:30:03.040
And then we both shot way way
way, way, way too much stuff,

00:30:03.040 --> 00:30:05.840
so Janessa kind of had
to pick the best chunk

00:30:05.840 --> 00:30:09.790
and see how they would fit
together, because she asked us

00:30:09.790 --> 00:30:11.950
to shoot maybe 2
minutes, and both of us

00:30:11.950 --> 00:30:13.450
gave her about 10
minutes of stuff.

00:30:13.450 --> 00:30:14.867
So I think we made
her job harder.

00:30:17.058 --> 00:30:18.100
What are you going to do?

00:30:18.100 --> 00:30:20.560
It was really-- it
ended up being really

00:30:20.560 --> 00:30:21.990
fun to dance around like that.

00:30:26.840 --> 00:30:28.420
Joy-- Yeah, I think--

00:30:28.420 --> 00:30:29.130
Oh, sorry.

00:30:29.130 --> 00:30:30.610
if Joy wants to go before us.

00:30:30.610 --> 00:30:31.830
OK, yes.

00:30:31.830 --> 00:30:36.960
I think for us, I guess I
mainly edited the video.

00:30:36.960 --> 00:30:40.560
So actually we had thought about
doing this concept beforehand

00:30:40.560 --> 00:30:44.970
and then I had no video
editing experience.

00:30:44.970 --> 00:30:48.360
And so I went and edited
another video instead,

00:30:48.360 --> 00:30:50.790
because I wanted to
make sure I knew stuff

00:30:50.790 --> 00:30:53.250
before going into this one.

00:30:53.250 --> 00:30:55.240
But I guess it was like
the process of finding,

00:30:55.240 --> 00:30:56.910
first of all, a
video editor where

00:30:56.910 --> 00:30:58.440
I could put in like
multiple screens

00:30:58.440 --> 00:31:01.870
and arrange them however I
wanted and stuff like that.

00:31:01.870 --> 00:31:04.800
And then after that, it was
just like a lot of googling

00:31:04.800 --> 00:31:10.650
to figure out how do I do
this or that [INAUDIBLE]

00:31:10.650 --> 00:31:12.150
and bring it all
together basically,

00:31:12.150 --> 00:31:13.500
because we had sort
of visually what

00:31:13.500 --> 00:31:15.833
we wanted it to look like
already mapped out beforehand.

00:31:20.020 --> 00:31:23.220
Joy, you want to jump in on
what ADT does with editing?

00:31:23.220 --> 00:31:23.720
Yeah.

00:31:23.720 --> 00:31:26.580
So back when we were
doing our covers,

00:31:26.580 --> 00:31:28.510
we used to film
many, many times.

00:31:28.510 --> 00:31:30.990
So I think we would
normally do nine or 10

00:31:30.990 --> 00:31:34.500
takes per cover, just like the
whole dance running through,

00:31:34.500 --> 00:31:37.980
which usually would take around
2 hours, 2 to 3 hours just

00:31:37.980 --> 00:31:38.580
for filming.

00:31:38.580 --> 00:31:40.990
Like filming days
are always big days.

00:31:40.990 --> 00:31:43.470
So if you remember the last
video I showed in the Premiere,

00:31:43.470 --> 00:31:44.928
we actually did
that during sunset,

00:31:44.928 --> 00:31:48.300
so that's why you saw the
sky literally change colors.

00:31:48.300 --> 00:31:50.640
And in terms of
editing, again it's

00:31:50.640 --> 00:31:52.590
the synchronization
tool on Premier Pro,

00:31:52.590 --> 00:31:55.620
definitely like holy
grail of dance editing,

00:31:55.620 --> 00:31:57.990
because you're able to
sync different things up

00:31:57.990 --> 00:31:59.640
really easily.

00:31:59.640 --> 00:32:01.380
And I'm also like
new to video editing.

00:32:01.380 --> 00:32:02.505
I just started this summer.

00:32:02.505 --> 00:32:06.030
I was making vlogs
for this art program

00:32:06.030 --> 00:32:09.060
that was happening also
with the arts office.

00:32:09.060 --> 00:32:12.390
So that's kind of how I was
training myself to prepare

00:32:12.390 --> 00:32:15.120
for this semester, because
I knew ADT was going

00:32:15.120 --> 00:32:16.753
to do a lot of video editing.

00:32:16.753 --> 00:32:17.680
Awesome.

00:32:17.680 --> 00:32:18.790
Thank you.

00:32:18.790 --> 00:32:20.470
So we just have
just a few minutes

00:32:20.470 --> 00:32:22.990
left, so I'm going to go with
the broadest question that's

00:32:22.990 --> 00:32:25.940
left is, what is the best
setup for virtual dancing?

00:32:25.940 --> 00:32:29.470
For example, is it better to
be closer to a computer mic

00:32:29.470 --> 00:32:32.500
or better to be far away to get
more of your body in the frame?

00:32:36.380 --> 00:32:38.580
I guess I can talk
about this a little bit.

00:32:38.580 --> 00:32:42.490
So for me and Faraaz, I guess
it depends on your goal.

00:32:42.490 --> 00:32:45.270
So we've been used
to dancing full body

00:32:45.270 --> 00:32:47.160
and having your entire
body in the frame,

00:32:47.160 --> 00:32:49.600
and like dancing in
a group and all that.

00:32:49.600 --> 00:32:51.480
So this is definitely
different, and I

00:32:51.480 --> 00:32:55.790
think we kind of went in
with the intention of just,

00:32:55.790 --> 00:32:58.940
you know, using
this kind of frame.

00:32:58.940 --> 00:33:03.430
So which meant like our face,
upper torso, and arms, I guess.

00:33:03.430 --> 00:33:05.835
And so in that case, it
had-- it worked out well,

00:33:05.835 --> 00:33:07.210
because we were
closer to the mic

00:33:07.210 --> 00:33:09.304
and basically
mimicked a Zoom setup.

00:33:15.600 --> 00:33:19.460
Joy or Dan, do you
guys want to chime in?

00:33:19.460 --> 00:33:25.990
I think for me what I do is
I actually have a USB mic,

00:33:25.990 --> 00:33:27.810
and I use--

00:33:27.810 --> 00:33:30.370
it has like almost
a 2-meter long cord,

00:33:30.370 --> 00:33:32.170
so what I normally
do is I put my laptop

00:33:32.170 --> 00:33:33.850
in the corner of
a room, and then

00:33:33.850 --> 00:33:36.700
I move the mic closer
to where I am teaching

00:33:36.700 --> 00:33:38.600
or standing or doing whatever.

00:33:38.600 --> 00:33:42.190
So my voice and the music,
which comes directly

00:33:42.190 --> 00:33:43.960
from the computer,
are both clear,

00:33:43.960 --> 00:33:45.915
but you can also
see my whole body.

00:33:45.915 --> 00:33:47.290
So that's what
works best for me.

00:33:50.000 --> 00:33:54.370
I think if there's a difference
between teaching and making

00:33:54.370 --> 00:33:56.050
for teaching.

00:33:56.050 --> 00:33:58.530
I've actually gotten--
like the best setup is

00:33:58.530 --> 00:34:02.170
a wireless Bluetooth
headset, I think,

00:34:02.170 --> 00:34:05.050
so that the sound is coming
through with your music,

00:34:05.050 --> 00:34:07.160
so there's not a
discrepancy between the two,

00:34:07.160 --> 00:34:09.235
and they're not
fighting each other.

00:34:09.235 --> 00:34:11.860
And there's a lot there's a lot
of different schools of thought

00:34:11.860 --> 00:34:12.790
and different setups.

00:34:12.790 --> 00:34:15.969
And as things get more advanced,
it becomes easier to teach.

00:34:15.969 --> 00:34:17.425
But the hardest
part for teaching

00:34:17.425 --> 00:34:19.300
is the balance between
you what you're saying

00:34:19.300 --> 00:34:22.210
and the music playing
and being in the room.

00:34:22.210 --> 00:34:26.347
In terms of virtual dancing,
for making something, I 100%

00:34:26.347 --> 00:34:27.639
agree with what Ani was saying.

00:34:27.639 --> 00:34:30.389
It's like what is your goal,
and do you want hands, do

00:34:30.389 --> 00:34:31.420
with the whole body?

00:34:31.420 --> 00:34:34.431
It's the same as like
setting things up on a stage.

00:34:34.431 --> 00:34:36.639
And shouldn't someone be in
the center, to the right,

00:34:36.639 --> 00:34:38.260
to the left You know, whatever.

00:34:38.260 --> 00:34:39.802
There's all sorts
of different stuff.

00:34:39.802 --> 00:34:42.824
It's case by case,
completely different.

00:34:42.824 --> 00:34:44.199
And there's really
cool stuff you

00:34:44.199 --> 00:34:47.409
can do with the virtual stuff.

00:34:47.409 --> 00:34:50.780
You can get like
a hand right here,

00:34:50.780 --> 00:34:54.250
which is really hard to do
in a proscenium theater.

00:34:54.250 --> 00:34:57.250
So you know, there's things
you can discover that make

00:34:57.250 --> 00:34:58.750
it even cooler to be on video.

00:34:58.750 --> 00:35:00.083
It's just sort of what you want.

00:35:02.150 --> 00:35:02.650
Awesome.

00:35:02.650 --> 00:35:04.430
We are at time for this panel.

00:35:04.430 --> 00:35:07.480
So I want to thank Faraaz,
Ani and Joy, and Dan

00:35:07.480 --> 00:35:08.510
for joining us.

00:35:08.510 --> 00:35:12.760
And I to thank the audience for
so much wonderful questions.

00:35:12.760 --> 00:35:14.650
Feel free, everybody,
to put your--

